  DEFENDING ARMY

  II Kahn led a host of loyal soldiers.  No generals were present to lead 
the forces of Uga-Buga Makumb.
  Kahn's soldiers, with no camels for the tortuous desert campaign, suffered 
from thirst.  The acting commander's soldiers, ill-supplied in the tortuous 
desert, suffered from thirst.  The battle was a fair fight, as no luck of 
any great importance appeared to affect the overall course of the engagement 
before it commenced.  In this perilous chess match, Kahn seemed to gain the 
tactical edge.

  Out on the left wing, the Veteran 220th Light Cavalry faced the 133rd 
Garrison.  On this field, the craven fear of a single soldier worked against 
the 133rd Garrison.  They were thrown into disarray when he ran, shouting 
"We're all cooked!"  The 220th Light Cavalry fought the 133rd Garrison and 
advanced at will.
  Out on the right outside flank, the Veteran 218th Legion were blessed by a 
band of priests sent by the League of Pagan Clerics, which inspired them to 
a fanatical zeal.  They were arrayed against the 166th Garrison.  The 188th 
Bowmen flanked the enemy and attacked with full force.  The 218th Legion 
fought the 166th Garrison and smashed their heads as if they were eggplants.
  Kahn's armies shattered the army opposing them with a final charge.  The 
220th Light Cavalry demolished the 133rd Garrison.  The 218th Legion 
scattered the 166th Garrison in all directions.
  Bir Nzaran was overrun by Kahn's forces in a bloody campaign.  The battle 
affected the quality of our forces:  the mayhem wreaked by the 188th Bowmen 
in the battle has raised the morale of its warriors, and all agree that it 
has risen to Veteran quality.  The acting commander withdrew to the Callisto 
Sea.
  Midsummer's night passed and a pleasant late summer came.  Kahn moved into 
Ravenwood (#1102) with his army.  To the west there is a Sea province known 
as the Giant Turtle Sea (#1002), an area of no races where no one reigns.  
Viewing the northwestern horizon, one can see a prosperous Sea province 
known as the Cannibal Sea (#1101).  It is said that no one reigns there.  It 
is known to be populated by Mermen.  If need be, they can call on an army of 
Mermen to defend their province.

  II Kahn's forces initiated a campaign of conquest in Ravenwood, a Forest 
province owing its allegiance to Shaman Uga-Buga Makumb Stuffy doll.  Kahn's 
forces conquered Ravenwood without any major engagements.  Ravenwood was 
found to have a tax base of 2 gold.

  Soldiers bearing the battle-standards of the Uga-Buga Makumbian empire 
marched on the Plain province of Damnonia, a land garrisoned by the forces 
of II Kahn.  Alaric (#22)'s forces encountered little resistance in Damnonia 
and Alaric there established our reign.  Alaric leads approximately 1 Bowmen,2
Legion and 1 Light Cavalry.
  With the advent of the fall season Kahn levied the 16th Legion from among 
the people of Ravenwood, to serve under Kahn.
  The fall season cooled into early winter.  Kahn invoked the Tome of 
Blessings, from which Milo's Crossroads was magically blessed and prospered. 
The Bless affected no armies in Milo's Crossroads.

  Kahn gained the Manual of Speedy Marches.  Lancelot went back to Valhalla, 
having tried his best to execute his assignments.

  Kahn was visited by a hero from Valhalla, named Sredna!  The 
characteristics for this great King from Game 57 are given in your 
"CHARACTERS" report.
  If Sredna searches a feature, any monetary treasure Sredna captures will 
be given to you.  If Sredna captures a magic treasure, Sredna will give it 
to your King when he departs for Valhalla, or you can order Sredna to Give 
it to one of your characters.  You can do anything you want with the magic 
item Sredna came with, but the item will leave at the end of his stay with 
you.  Except for the rules given in this paragraph, Sredna can generally be 
treated just like your other Heroes, and can be affected by any spell except 
Resurrection.  The main limitation in using Sredna is that Sredna has only 
enough patience to practice with you for one of the phase(s) Sredna is with 
you.  Sredna will leave at the end of the last phase Sredna is scheduled to 
serve you, after all battles have been fought.  Sredna can lead armies, but 
when Sredna returns to Valhalla, the armies will normally be left leaderless 
in the province they are in.  If Sredna has the armies in a province you 
don't own, they will be transferred to your lowest-numbered leader in the 
same province, or at last resort, the garrison commander in an adjacent 
province owned by you.  If no leader is available, the armies will be 
disbanded.

  Thus endeth the adventures of II Kahn for this year.


  Kahn treated with his ally Bradipo I (#2).  Kahn treated with his ally 
Cosmic Herderer Mugai (#3).  Kahn treated with his ally Hokuto (#4).  Kahn 
treated with his ally Prometeo (#5).  Kahn treated with his ally 
Giovanna d'Arco (#7).  Kahn treated with his ally Circe (#8).  Kahn treated 
with his ally Agesilao (#9).

  Here is the financial report for Kahn's kingdom: this turn, the Kingdom 
collected 16 gold in revenue.  3 gold was expended to finance our leaders.  
The 16th Legion trained well and improved in quality.  9 gold was expended 
to maintain our armies.  Kahn was left with 29 gold in the treasury.


LETTER REPORTS

  O King of Kings, thy heroes have travelled around the globe in
search of adventure and conquest.  I have faithfully kept their
correspondence, and now I present it to thee:


From: Lancelot
To: Kahn

  Honored Sovereign, 

  With the advent of the spring season, I set out to quench my thirst for 
adventure.  I wandered in search of Gladstone (#71).  I travelled to Mercia. 
Southeast of here is Oceanus Cyclopia (#1405), a Sea province ruled by 
Shaman Uga-Buga Makumb Stuffy doll, where no races dwell.  I saw no 
wandering monsters.
  During early summer, I travelled in search of Gladstone.  I journeyed to 
Quantara Roo.  I caught up with Gladstone in Quantara Roo.  I scouted for 
Gladstone in the province of Quantara Roo.  I could not encounter Gladstone 
because Gladstone led an army and I didn't.  My efforts raised my prowess in 
Spying.  In my travels, I saw no wandering monster.
  Midsummer's night came and went and late summer came.  I downed a nervous 
draught from my bottle of scotch, and entered the Sacred Rock.  I 
encountered the Sphinx.  Waving my hands, I orchestrated otherworldly forces 
of magic, casting a Psychic Blast spell, projecting psychic waves of 
stunning force towards the Sphinx.  The Sphinx's spell was disrupted by the 
event.  The Sphinx stood dazed and perplexed, stunned out of its mind.  I 
chanted my strange words of magic and cast a Telekinetic Storm spell and 
scored 15 damage points on the Sphinx.  Some fragments zipped towards me and 
inflicted 2 damage points.  The Sphinx was stunned and staggered around at 
random.  Chanting my artful words of magic into a great Spiritwrack spell, I 
gestured with my hands, to make the Sphinx quake and shiver with spiritual 
convulsions.  Stunned, the Sphinx could only shake its head in perplexity.
  Casting a Holy Symbol spell, I directed glowing, writhing strands of magic.
A golden mist in the shape of a cross formed around me.  The Sphinx stood 
stunned, unable to regain its senses.
  I loped along steadily and closed to bow range.  The effects of the stun 
wore off and the Sphinx prepared to renew its attack.  I boldly shot an 
arrow at the Sphinx and would have hit the Sphinx if the Sphinx hadn't 
ducked just then.  I shot an arrow at the Sphinx and damaged the Sphinx, 
inflicting 2 damage points.

  The Sphinx charged into the melee.  I maneuvered for a good attacking 
position.  Then I swang at the Sphinx with all my might and inflicted 4 
damage points.  Hoping to exploit a gap in my defenses, the Sphinx snapped 
at me but I dodged the blow.  With surprising speed, I swang at the Sphinx 
but my aim was slightly off.
  The Sphinx feigned panic, then pivoted and snapped at me and missed.  I 
flamboyantly flipped over the Sphinx and deftly thrust at the Sphinx but my 
sword whiffed through the air.  The Sphinx snapped at me and would have 
struck me if I hadn't ducked just then.  I summoned all my will and bravely 
swang at my vaunted enemy but the Sphinx evaded the blow.
  The Sphinx lunged at me but its aim was slightly off the mark.  Showing no 
fear, I thrust at the Sphinx and missed badly.  The Sphinx attacked me but 
its jaws whiffed through the air.  I turned back the Sphinx with my 
aggressive tactics.  I then swang at the Sphinx with maximum force and would 
have hit the Sphinx if the Sphinx hadn't ducked.
  I shifted over to a good defensive vantage.  Then I thrust at the Sphinx 
but the Sphinx evaded the sword.  Hoping to take advantage of a weak point 
in Lancelot's defenses, the Sphinx skillfully lunged at me but its aim was 
just off.  With surprising speed, the Sphinx lunged at me and scored on me, 
inflicting 1 points of damage.  I thrust at the Sphinx and missed by a mile. 
I contemptuously swang at the Sphinx but my sword whiffed harmlessly through 
the air.  The Sphinx viciously snapped at me and would have struck me if I 
hadn't ducked just then.
  I summoned all my strength and bravely swang at my fearsome nemesis but 
the Sphinx evaded the blow.  The Sphinx audaciously lunged at me and 
walloped me with 1 damage points.  Showing no fear, I moved closer and swang 
at the Sphinx and inflicted 2 damage points on the Sphinx.  The Sphinx 
angrily attacked me but its aim was just off.  I turned back the Sphinx with 
my aggressive assault.  I then thrust at the Sphinx with terrific force and 
damaged the Sphinx, scoring 3 damage points.  The Sphinx snapped at me with 
all its strength and missed by a mile.
  I deftly thrust at the Sphinx and inflicted 5 damage points.  The Sphinx 
staggered, stunned by the deadly blow.  I cleared out the treasure of 
the Sphinx.  I secured the Manual of Speedy Marches.  I examined the Manual 
of Speedy Marches and discovered the following properties:
  The Manual of Speedy Marches raises your Forced March ability by one 
level.  For example, a skill level of 0 would rise to skill level 1.  The 
reader of this strangely compelling work becomes completely engrossed with 
it, and after this book is perused, it crumbles to dust and merges with the 
sands of time.  Use the "Use Item" Action to invoke its powers.
  I looted 6 gold from the treasure trove of the Sphinx.  I knelt beside the 
Dais.
  Magic runes formed on the Dais, saying "By the power of the Dais, I zap 
you from female to male!  How do you like the improvement?  Har de har har!  !!"
During my travels this season, I was crossing an open field when a 
Renegade Psychic spotted me from a distance.  But I spoke to 
the Renegade Psychic with friendly words, and combat was avoided.

  During fall, I could not search the Tomb because it is in another 
province.  Instead, I discovered nothing worthy of note in my sagely 
researches.  While adventuring, I encountered no monsters.
  The fall season cooled into a frigid early winter.  I travelled in search 
of Guerin (#18).  I caught up with Guerin in Quantara Roo.  I scouted for 
Guerin in Quantara Roo.  I could not encounter Guerin because Guerin led an 
army and I didn't.
  Kahn gained the Manual of Speedy Marches.  I went back to Valhalla, having 
tried my best to execute my assignments.


  Thy humble servant,   Lancelot


From: Frodo, Squire to Hawkins
To: Kahn

  Honored Sovereign, 

  Stormy winter melted into spring, and Hawkins prepared to carry out his 
urgent assignments.  He recruited enough men in Bronzeleaf to form the 63rd 
Legion for the army of Hawkins.
  With the advent of the early summer season Hawkins's Oratory spell 
backfired and Hawkins wound up covered with psycho-kinetic goo.
  In late summer, Hawkins's attempted cast of the Phantasmal Armies spell 
was unsuccessful.

  An army coming from the empire of Shaman Uga-Buga Makumb Stuffy doll (#1) 
invaded Bronzeleaf, hoping to subjugate that province owned by II Kahn and 
made up of great Forests.  Odysseus (#97) was in command of the attacking 
armies.  The defending forces were led by Hawkins.  The armies were drawn up 
into lines of battle as shown here:

  ATTACKING ARMY, Battle of Bronzeleaf (1303):

  123rd     102nd             
  Eagles    Eagles            
